00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,879
What first-level analysis means.

2
00:00:01,879 --> 00:00:07,517
Connect one participant's bold time series to a model of one run.

3
00:00:07,517 --> 00:00:13,155
The goal is clear: Distinguish first-level analysis from within-subject and group analysis.

4
00:00:14,005 --> 00:00:15,732
What first-level analysis means.

5
00:00:15,732 --> 00:00:20,050
First-level analysis models a single run from a single participant.

6
00:00:20,050 --> 00:00:28,685
It estimates how well the predicted response for each explanatory variable accounts for the observed bold signal at every voxel.

7
00:00:28,685 --> 00:00:31,707
The output is not a group conclusion.

8
00:00:31,707 --> 00:00:40,342
It is a set of participant/run-specific parameter estimates and contrasts that can later feed Level 2 or Level 3 models.

9
00:00:40,342 --> 00:00:44,227
Keep the unit of analysis explicit when naming outputs.

10
00:00:45,077 --> 00:00:48,643
Now connect the idea to an observable check.

11
00:00:48,643 --> 00:00:52,654
First, choose one bold run in a BIDS dataset.

12
00:00:52,654 --> 00:00:56,220
Next, identify its participant, task, and run entities.

13
00:00:56,220 --> 00:01:00,677
Then, sketch where its first-level dot feat output should live.

14
00:01:00,677 --> 00:01:04,688
Record what you observe so the decision remains reviewable.

15
00:01:05,538 --> 00:01:08,160
Quality control comes before interpretation.

16
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:16,549
Before interpreting the result, confirm that the input, operation, and output support the same scientific question.

17
00:01:16,549 --> 00:01:25,986
A useful self-check is this: In one or two sentences, what decision does “What first-level analysis means” support?

18
00:01:25,986 --> 00:01:30,180
Distinguish first-level analysis from within-subject and group analysis.

19
00:01:31,030 --> 00:01:38,146
To recap: First, connect one participant's bold time series to a model of one run.

20
00:01:38,146 --> 00:01:43,364
Next, document the decision and inspect the corresponding input or output.

21
00:01:43,364 --> 00:01:51,429
Before continuing, make sure you can explain this objective: Distinguish first-level analysis from within-subject and group analysis.
